 ### Recent builds and share links
 
-`/builds` (linked from the form) lists the builds of the last `RDGEN_RETENTION_DAYS` with their files and status. For a finished build it makes a **share link**: `/share/<token>` shows the build's files and needs no login, so you can send a client to someone without putting it on a public page. The token is 32 random bytes. A link expires after 1, 7 or 30 days, and never later than the build itself; you can delete it sooner. Each link counts its downloads.
+`/builds` (linked from the form) lists the builds of the last `RDGEN_RETENTION_DAYS` with their files and status. **Delete** removes a finished or failed build at once: its files and share links go, and it disappears from the list, the status pages and the API. A hand-off to the console that hasn't run yet is skipped, and `rdgen_publish mark` prints `deleted` for such a build instead of recording anything. The row stays until the usual pruning, so a failed run on GitHub is still deleted on schedule. Copies already in the console stay there. For a finished build it makes a **share link**: `/share/<token>` shows the build's files and needs no login, so you can send a client to someone without putting it on a public page. The token is 32 random bytes. A link expires after 1, 7 or 30 days, and never later than the build itself; you can delete it sooner. Each link counts its downloads.
 
 Links point at `RDGEN_SHARE_BASE` when it is set (e.g. `https://console.example.com/share`). Otherwise they point at rdgen itself, which is normally reachable on the LAN only. A reverse proxy on that public host has to pass exactly `/share/<43 characters>` and `/share/<43 characters>/<file>` to rdgen, GET only, and nothing else. The RustDesk LXC's console does that.
 
